<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
      The identity module's behaviour, against a real in-process Orleans cluster.

      The suite is organised around docs/plan/11's failure classes rather than around its types,
      because those are the things that are easy to get subtly wrong and impossible to notice:

        EnumerationAndTimingTests   — the same response AND the same time, both measured
        LockoutIsGrainFreeTests     — the failure path touches no grain, asserted against a
                                      recording grain factory rather than argued
        RefreshReuseTests           — a replayed refresh kills the CHAIN, not the request
        EmailUniquenessTests        — per tenant, not global, in both directions
        PiiNeverReachesALogMessageTests — a real sign-in with a distinctive address, and a capturing
                                      logger
        CredentialTests             — Argon2id round-trip and PHC parsing, RFC 6238 test vectors,
                                      recovery codes
        GroupMembershipTests        — membership is tuples: nesting works, and the grain holds no list

      ⚠ NO Testcontainers AND NO Aspire.Hosting, AND THAT IS A DEVIATION FROM ADR-018 THAT IS OWED
      BACK — the same one CyberCloud.ResourceManager.Tests records. ADR-018 wants grain behaviour
      tested "against a real in-process TestCluster … with real storage providers backed by
      Testcontainers"; this uses AddMemoryGrainStorage because the environment this was built in
      could not start a container.

      What that costs here specifically, stated so it can be paid rather than forgotten:

        - Nothing here proves UserGrainState or SessionGrainState SERIALIZE. In-memory storage keeps
          the object graph; a real provider round-trips it, which is where the "STJ does not populate
          a get-only collection" trap bites. Every collection on both is `{ get; set; }` for that
          reason, and CyberCloud.Identity.Contracts.Tests round-trips the WIRE types through a real
          Orleans serializer — but the two STATE types are owed a storage-backed assertion.
        - The lockout counter under test is InMemoryLockoutCounter, not RedisLockoutCounter. What is
          proven is the property that matters — the failure path takes no grain reference — and the
          ladder's arithmetic. What is not proven is that the Redis INCR/EXPIRE pair behaves as
          described on a real cluster, which needs a Redis.
        - The timing tests run Argon2id at REDUCED parameters (see EnumerationAndTimingTests). That
          is deliberate and does not weaken the assertion: lowering the cost lowers BOTH branches
          identically, and the property under test is that the two are equal.
    -->

    <ItemGroup>
        <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Shouldly"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Orleans.TestingHost"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Orleans.Multitenant"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup Label="Global usings">
        <Using Include="Xunit"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Identity/CyberCloud.Identity.csproj"/>
        <!-- The REAL email index grain — per-tenant uniqueness is tested against it, not against a fake. -->
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Tenancy/CyberCloud.Tenancy.csproj"/>
        <!-- The REAL tuple store and check grain — group membership is tested through the engine. -->
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Authorization/CyberCloud.Authorization.csproj"/>
        <!--
          The REAL sending domain, not a fake IMessageSender — OtpDeliveryTests is about the
          idempotency key reaching a real IMessageGrain, and a double for IMessageSender would assert
          only that this suite's own stub counts calls. This is the implementation assembly rather
          than its contracts because the test needs AddCyberCloudCommunication, GrainMessageSender
          and InMemoryChannelProvider. ⚠ CyberCloud.Identity itself references only
          CyberCloud.Communication.Contracts; see its .csproj for why the direction matters.
        -->
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Communication/CyberCloud.Communication.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
